PKIMaster for Debian 13
======================

Install the package with:
  sudo apt install ./pkimaster_0.4.0-1_all.deb

Open https://127.0.0.1:8443/setup on the server, or forward the console from
your workstation:
  ssh -L 8443:127.0.0.1:8443 your-server

The automatically generated localhost certificate is self-signed. Complete
the first administrator setup, then use the Settings page to choose the HTTPS
listener address/port and upload a trusted server certificate and private key.
Listener/TLS changes are picked up automatically within a few seconds and
restart the HTTPS workers. This can briefly interrupt browser connections.
No environment secrets or human-edited configuration files are required.

Runtime state, including encrypted CA keys, generated encryption secrets,
accounts and audit events, lives in /var/lib/pkimaster (mode 0700). Use Backup
& restore for encrypted snapshots and localhost recovery on a fresh host.
For manual filesystem copies stop the web service and publication/monitoring/automation
timers and workers, and copy the entire directory consistently; the database
alone is insufficient to recover encrypted keys. State and the dedicated
_pkimaster system account are retained on both package removal and purge.

The monitoring timer checks expiry and configured public CRLs every five
minutes. Configure SMTP or webhook delivery under Monitoring & alerts.
See BACKUP.md and MONITORING.md in /usr/share/doc/pkimaster for procedures.

The automation timer handles configured parent CRL synchronization, encrypted
SFTP backups and external audit archives. Configure Automation and keep the
downloaded private recovery key separately. See AUTOMATION.md for recovery.
ACME enrollment is opt-in and requires an explicitly scoped certificate
template and external account binding. See ACME.md for client setup.

The service runs as _pkimaster with systemd sandboxing. Logs are available via:
  journalctl -u pkimaster

The supported package target is Debian 13 (trixie). Dependencies are provided
by APT; package installation never invokes pip or downloads Python packages.
